## Check your phone, country and age

The help pages are more specific than the announcements, and they matter more than a headline.

**Multi-step tasks** are a beta called screen automation. The help page lists Pixel 10, 10 Pro, 10 Pro XL, 11, 11 Pro, 11 Pro XL and 11 Pro Fold, the Galaxy S26 series, Z Flip 8, Z Fold 8 and Tab S12 series, and the Motorola Signature 27. That list omits the Pixel 10 Pro Fold, which the Gemini Intelligence post includes in this update, and it does not include the Pixel 10a. Check your phone before relying on it. You must be 18 or older and use a personal Google account; work, school and supervised accounts are excluded. Countries are India, Japan, South Korea, the UK and the US, but Pixel devices are not supported in South Korea. Languages are English, Japanese, Korean and Spanish, in select apps. Rollout is gradual, and there are caps on daily requests and on tasks running at once. Buying and selling, travel bookings and ticket purchases are listed as starting with the Galaxy Z Flip 8 and Z Fold 8, so do not assume your phone has them.

**Custom widgets** on Pixel require a Pixel 10 or newer, excluding the 10a, and a user aged 18 or over. The help page says English only, in Australia, Canada, India, the UK and the US. It also says Connected Apps are not available in the UK, so widgets that draw on your personal data may be more limited there. Some features may need a subscription for higher usage; the page gives no figures.

**Rambler** is where the pages disagree. Both 10 November posts put Rambler on Pixel 10 (the Gemini Intelligence post also names the motorola signature 27), in select countries and languages. The Pixel help page we read on 26 November says Pixel 11 series only. The help page may simply lag behind the rollout, but we cannot confirm that. Look for Rambler under Gboard's voice typing settings. The help page lists Arabic, English, French, German, Hindi and other Indian languages, Italian, Japanese, Korean, Portuguese, Russian and Spanish, and says usage limits apply. It also asks for the latest Gboard set as the default keyboard, microphone permission, and an internet connection for full features.

## Safety and privacy before you hand over a task

Google's own help page is blunt about screen automation. It is a beta, it can make mistakes such as choosing the wrong item or quantity, placing an unauthorised order or wrongly reporting success, and you are responsible for what it does, including purchases. It also warns that instructions hidden in web or app content (known as prompt injection) could trick the agent into placing orders, changing addresses or sending messages. Google says its safeguards do not guarantee protection.

Practical steps drawn from that page:

1. If Gemini shows an action plan, read it before confirming, and complete the final confirmation or order yourself.
2. Do not type passwords or card details into the chat. Take control and enter them in the app.
3. Watch the task while it runs. Do not use it for emergencies or tasks involving sensitive information.
4. Before sharing a chat that used screen automation, check it. Sharing creates a public link that anyone can view.

On Pixel 10 and Galaxy S26 the help page says you cannot take control during a task, only stop it, using Stop response and then Stop task, or the live notification. That means step 2's take-control route is not available on those phones, so avoid tasks there that involve passwords or payment details midway.

Google's Gemini privacy page also says screenshots are taken during screen automation and can include anything visible in the app. It says screenshots are reviewed by trained reviewers and used to improve Google services if the setting it calls History is on. Google also says it may hold reviewed chats and linked data for as long as three years, and deleting them does not shorten that. Turning History off does not mean nothing is kept: the page says future chats are still saved for around two months. It also gives 18 months as the default auto-delete period for History.

For Rambler, the help page says your text, audio and voice edits are processed by Google temporarily, are not saved, stored or shared, and are deleted once the text is delivered. This is general information, not legal advice.

## How to switch things off

- **Screen automation:** in the Gemini app tap Menu, your profile picture or initial, Settings, Settings again, then Screen automation. Each app can be set to Always allow, Ask every time or Do not allow.
- **Rambler:** open Settings from the Gboard toolbar, choose Voice typing and select Standard voice typing, which is plain word-for-word dictation.
- **Everything else:** Android Help says to manage features and data permissions from device settings. It does not describe one master switch.

## What this means for a small business

If you run a business from a work Google account, note that screen automation is not offered on it. Owners who use a personal account for bookings or orders should treat the agent like a new assistant: start with low-value tasks, check each order, and keep tasks that need passwords or card details out of the agent on phones where you cannot take control. If your staff are in a country or language not listed above for that feature, assume the feature is unavailable until your phone shows it.
